course: dictionary
lesson: 13503
title: Strong words: immensely
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Immensely is an adverb. It tells you how much, and it makes another word much stronger. «Mia was immensely proud of her science project.» Proud is the feeling, and immensely tells you how proud: extremely proud, very much.
explain: Immensely can also go with a verb. «We enjoyed the concert immensely.» This means we enjoyed it very much. To check, swap in very much and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Writers often use immensely in stories and formal writing, where it sounds rich and grown-up. In everyday talk, people usually say «really» or «very» instead.
fact: Immensely means extremely; very much, to a great degree.
fact: Immensely is an adverb, so it tells us more about an adjective or a verb.
fact: «Immensely popular» means very popular.
fact: «Enjoyed it immensely» means enjoyed it very much.
fact: In everyday talk, use «really» or «very» instead of immensely.
exercise: «Mia was immensely proud of her science project.» What does immensely mean here? | Quietly | *Very much | Once a year | Slightly
exercise: Which words could replace immensely in «We enjoyed the film immensely»? | Not at all | A little bit | *Very much | In a hurry
exercise: «Grandad was immensely proud when I won.» What does immensely tell us about the pride? | *It shows the pride is very strong | It shows the pride is in the past | It shows the pride is secret | It shows the pride is gone
test: «The old castle is immensely popular with tourists.» Immensely means | *Extremely, very much | Sometimes | Quickly | Not at all
test: Which everyday word could replace immensely in «I enjoyed the book immensely»? | Badly | Carefully | *Really | Never
exam: «The huge waves were immensely powerful.» What does immensely mean here? | In a quiet way | *Extremely, very much | A little bit | Hardly at all
